# Contribute what this repository learned about a layer

Send a change or a complaint back to the layer's maintainer, with the evidence
attached.

## When

A layer's output was edited here and the edit is still standing; a check the layer
ships cannot be satisfied; a default was overridden and stayed overridden; somebody
asks whether a local fix should go upstream. Also on any deliberate periodic sweep —
`ilk contribute status` shows every layer with something to say.

Not for a change to ilk itself, and not for a layer this repository authors. Editing
a layer you own is just editing it.

## What ilk already knows

Do not gather this by hand. `ilk contribute <layer>` reads it from what ilk recorded
when it applied the layer:

- every artifact of the layer that no longer matches what the layer delivered, as a
  diff against the layer's **own source path** — not against where the file landed
  here
- whether that divergence was *agreed*: somebody was shown both versions and kept
  this one
- how long it has held, in commits, and how many times the repository has come back
  to the same file
- defaults overridden, checks failing, exemptions never cleared, artifacts deleted

## What ilk cannot know

Two sections are left marked `TODO(you):` and submission refuses while they stand.
They are the whole contribution; the diff is the easy part.

**What this repository needed.** The situation, not the patch. What were you trying
to do, and what did the layer do instead? A maintainer can read a diff and cannot
read your afternoon.

**Why this is not specific to this repository.** The load-bearing one. Why would
another adopter hit this? If the honest answer is that they would not, say exactly
that and send it anyway — a layer being wrong for one repository in a way that
repository can articulate is far more useful than silence. A maintainer who receives
three of those about the same variable has learned something no single diff carries.

Write both. Then:

```sh
ilk contribute <layer> --submit
```

## Read the layer's own guidelines

`ilk contribute` prints them if the layer ships any. They are the maintainer's
standard, shipped inside the layer precisely so you have it before writing rather
than in review.

## Replayable and not

A patch marked **replayable** applies to the layer's source unchanged: nothing was
templated into the file. One that is not carries this repository's values — a
directory name, a project number — and a maintainer who applied it would take those
upstream too. That is not a failure of the proposal; it means the evidence is the
contribution and the change gets made by hand at the other end. Say what you meant,
in the prose.

## When it refuses

Submission stops on anything that looks like a credential. A proposal is public and
git history is permanent, so this is not a warning to override — remove it from the
file, rotate it, and run again.

It also stops when the layer declares no `contribution:` block. That is the layer
saying it does not take proposals; there is nowhere to send one, and opening an
issue by hand is the fallback.

## The other direction

This is half a loop. `ilk upgrade` is the other half: when the maintainer acts on a
proposal, the improvement comes back down and three-way merges into every repository
that had tuned the layer — including the tuning that prompted it. A proposal accepted
upstream is how a local fix stops being a local fix.
